    <desc>Get notified when a long command finishes, or when a terminal prints something or goes quiet.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Notifications for commands and activity</title>

<section id="commands">
    <title>When a long command finishes</title>
    <p>When a command that ran for a while finishes in a terminal you are not
    looking at, <app>Pano Terminal</app> sends a notification with the command,
    whether it succeeded, and how long it took, and marks its tab. Click the
    notification to go to the terminal.</p>
    <p>This needs <link xref="cmd-shell-integration">shell integration</link>.
    To change it:</p>
    <steps>
<item><p>Press the menu button in the top-right corner of the window and select <gui style="menuitem">Preferences</gui>.</p></item>
<item><p>In the <gui>Behavior</gui> group, turn <gui>Notify When Commands Finish</gui> on or off, and set in <gui>Minimum Command Duration</gui> how many seconds a command must run. The default is 10.</p></item>
</steps>
  </section>

  <section id="activity">
    <title>Activity and silence</title>
    <p>You can also watch a terminal for output, for example a download or a
    build in another tab. Right-click the terminal and select:</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui style="menuitem">Monitor for Activity</gui></title>
        <p>Marks the tab and notifies you when the terminal prints
        something.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui style="menuitem">Monitor for Silence</gui></title>
        <p>Marks the tab and notifies you when the terminal has printed nothing
        for a while, by default for 10 seconds. Set how long in
        <gui>Silence Duration</gui> in the <gui>Behavior</gui> group of the
        preferences.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>This works with any program, without shell integration, and only while
    you are not looking at the terminal.</p>
  </section>

